.th MM VII "1 July 77"
.SH NAME
mm \*- macros for making manuals
.sh SYNOPSIS
nroff -\fBmm\fR [options] file ...
.br
troff -\fBmm\fR [options] file ...
.sh DESCRIPTION
This package of
.it nroff
and
.it troff
macro definitions is used for producing the manual entries in
the "Unix Programmer's Manual".
.sp
The macros supplied are:
.lp +6 5
\&.th	Enter the title/heading.
Needs three arguments.
.br
e.g.
.ti +5
\&.th APROG I 77/2/11
.br
where the arguments are:
1) the program/package name.
2) the section number in upper-case roman numerals.
3) the date of last edit (year/month/day at CSU).
.sp
.lp +6 5
\&.sh	Produce a subheading.
.br
e.g.
.ti +5
\&.sh NAME
.br
The standard subheadings are: NAME, SYNOPSIS, DESCRIPTION, FILES,
"SEE ALSO", DIAGNOSTICS, AUTHOR and BUGS.
.sp
.lp +6 5
\&.bd	Prints the single argument in bold font
(if
.it nroff
instead of
.it troff
it simply underlines the argument).
.br
e.g.
.ti +5
\&.bd bold
.sp
.lp +6 5
\&.it	Prints the single argument in italics.
(underlines if \fInroff\fR)
.sp
.lp +6 5
\&.lp	Labelled paragraph.
Common usage is: ".lp +6 5".
This example sets the left margin 6 spaces to the right of its
previous position and places the label 5 positions to the left of
that position.
When all labelled paragraphs in a group have been entered, then
two more macros are used:
.sp
.lp +12 5
\&.i0	reset the indentation origin.
(note the zero in the name)
.sp
.lp +12 5
\&.dt	define tabs.
.sp
.lp +6 5
\&.s1
.lp +6 5
\&.s2
.lp +6 5
\&.s3	Do some (unknown) amount of spacing.
The actual amount does not seem to be constant between
some the sets of macros.
Usually, only "\fI.s3\fR" is used.
.sp
.i0
.dt
For those people who do not have
.it troff
manuals, here is a very
brief description of two features that are commonly used in
.it troff
programs and ignored by the
.it nroff
processor.
"\\fX" is a
.it troff
command used to change the font.
`X' is a single character used to specify the appropriate font.
The standard ones are: R(roman, normal text), I(italics), B(bold).
Another common command is "\\sn" which is used to set the size
of the print font used.
`n' is the size of the font used in characters per inch.
i.e. "\\s10" is used to set the size to pica (10 characters to
the inch), which is the default size used.
One interesting convention used in the manuals is that
the word "unix" is printed in upper-case of a smaller size.
The way this is done is: "\\s8UNIX\\s10".
The reason for these descriptions is that sometimes it is
found that the ".it" and ".bd" macros are not sufficiently
powerful.
.sh FILES
.lp +18 18
/usr/lib/tmac.m	the standard macro file
.lp +18 18
man/man0/[knt]aa	files supplied on the \s8UNIX\s10
documentation disk. "naa" is the \fInroff\fR version and "taa" the
\fItroff\fR version.
.i0
.dt
.sh "SEE ALSO"
nroff(I), troff(I)
